{-
   What a generic body compiled **once** refuses at its own calls.

   Separate from `Reject.Erasure.yana` for the reason that file gives: these are reported while the
   call graph is walked, which only happens once every form has been chosen. Both of them are limits
   of the environment rather than mistakes in the program - each names a call that is perfectly well
   typed and that the erased ABI has no way to hand anything over for.

   There was a second shape here and it is `Erased.Superclass.yana` now: a member reached through a
   **superclass** of a declared requirement, which a flattened witness table answers.
-}

data Vessel {size: I32}

fn (a) helper(x: a) -> I32 = 2 :: I32

{-
   A generic called at this body's own type parameters.

   An environment is built by the **caller**, out of answers it has: a call from a concrete body
   knows every argument, so every slot is a type, a number or the address of a table. Here the
   argument is `a`, and what would have to be written into the callee's slots is what stands in this
   body's own - a forwarding, and a table in the image cannot forward. What it needs is an
   environment built on the frame out of loads from this one, which is a generalization of
   `environmentFor` rather than a different mechanism, and it is the honest remaining gap.
-}
@generic
fn (a.size: I32) onward(x: a) -> I32 = helper(x) + x.size

fn main() -> I32:
    let v = Vessel {size: 1 :: I32}
    return onward(v)
